RV32I Level 4 control-flow and corner-case test
================================================

Memory image: tb/mem/control_flow_corner_cases_test.mem
Each line is one 32-bit instruction, starting at byte address 0.

Coverage:
  - Taken and not-taken conditional branches.
  - A branch immediately dependent on a just-produced register value.
  - JAL and JALR redirects, link-address values, and wrong-path suppression.
  - JALR bit-zero clearing: x9 contains odd address 0x59; the core must jump
    to 0x58 after clearing bit 0.
  - x0 remains zero after an instruction attempts to write it.
  - Interactions between branches/jumps and dependent instructions.
  - Reset-entry assumption: the external testbench must assert reset before
    releasing the core to begin fetching at PC=0.

Result signature:
  dmem[64] = 0x00000055 : checks passed
  dmem[64] = 0x000000EE : a check failed
The signature is written to byte address 0x100. The program then parks.

Reset and alignment scope:
  Reset is an input to the core, not an instruction; this ROM alone cannot
  pulse reset or verify asynchronous reset assertion. It checks operation from
  the reset entry state (PC=0, x0=0), assuming the testbench asserts reset.
  The JALR case verifies clearing address bit 0 for an odd base address that
  resolves to an aligned instruction address. The core has no trap/exception
  path, so this image does not test misaligned-instruction trap handling.

Address    Machine code  Instruction / purpose
--------   ------------  ---------------------
00000000   00500093      addi x1,x0,5                ; x1 = 5
00000004   00500113      addi x2,x0,5                ; x2 = 5
00000008   00700193      addi x3,x0,7                ; expected value preserved across redirects
0000000c   00208463      beq x1,x2,+8                ; taken; skip 0x10
00000010   06300193      addi x3,x0,99               ; wrong path; must be flushed
00000014   00700213      addi x4,x0,7                ; branch target
00000018   06419663      bne x3,x4,fail              ; confirms BEQ redirect flushed wrong path
0000001c   06209463      bne x1,x2,fail              ; not taken because x1 == x2
00000020   00500313      addi x6,x0,5                ; value produced immediately before branch
00000024   00130463      beq x6,x1,+8                ; taken; branch depends on preceding ADDI
00000028   06300193      addi x3,x0,99               ; wrong path; must be flushed
0000002c   00700213      addi x4,x0,7                ; branch target
00000030   04419a63      bne x3,x4,fail              ; confirms dependent branch redirect
00000034   0080046f      jal x8,+8                   ; jump to 0x3c; x8 gets link 0x38
00000038   06300193      addi x3,x0,99               ; wrong path after JAL
0000003c   03800213      addi x4,x0,0x38             ; expected JAL link address
00000040   04441263      bne x8,x4,fail              ; verify JAL wrote PC+4
00000044   00700213      addi x4,x0,7
00000048   02419e63      bne x3,x4,fail              ; verify JAL flushed wrong path
0000004c   05900493      addi x9,x0,0x59             ; odd JALR base; target is 0x58
00000050   00048567      jalr x10,0(x9)              ; masks bit 0, jumps to 0x58; link=0x54
00000054   06300193      addi x3,x0,99               ; wrong path after JALR
00000058   05400213      addi x4,x0,0x54             ; expected JALR link address
0000005c   02451463      bne x10,x4,fail             ; verify JALR wrote PC+4
00000060   00700213      addi x4,x0,7
00000064   02419063      bne x3,x4,fail              ; verify JALR flushed wrong path
00000068   06308013      addi x0,x1,99               ; attempt to write x0; result discarded
0000006c   00000213      addi x4,x0,0                ; read x0
00000070   00021a63      bne x4,x0,fail              ; x0 must still be zero
00000074   10000593      addi x11,x0,256             ; result address = byte address 0x100
00000078   05500613      addi x12,x0,0x55            ; PASS signature
0000007c   00c5a023      sw x12,0(x11)               ; write PASS to dmem[64]
00000080   00c0006f      jal x0,+12                  ; skip FAIL handler
00000084   0ee00613      addi x12,x0,0xEE            ; FAIL signature
00000088   00c5a023      sw x12,0(x11)               ; write FAIL to dmem[64]
0000008c   0000006f      jal x0,0                   ; park forever

The core does not implement trap handling for misaligned instruction addresses.
The JALR test therefore checks the implemented bit-0 masking behavior with a
target that is word-aligned after masking.
